    stylePrompt: "Bureaucratic bossa nova / clipboard lounge, 96 BPM, D minor drifting to F major on every chorus like a form being approved. Nylon-string guitar comping the classic bossa pattern, brushed snare, soft upright bass, a single vibraphone that only plays on the word \"permit\". One office-printer rhythm buried in the percussion — feed roller, page drop — treated as a shaker. Female lead voice, warm, bored, reading minutes aloud with perfect diction; she never raises her volume, only her eyebrows. Two-voice deadpan backing on the chorus (Lillith and Rubella, non-voting, tight unison at the back of the room). Bridge is the roll-call: spoken, dry mic, a pen clicking on 2 and 4. Mood: the morning after the portal, coffee still warm, cosmic horror reclassified as seasonal décor. No horns, no samba drums, no jokes in the arrangement — the arrangement is the straight man. End on an unresolved Dm add9 with the printer finishing a page.",
    lyrics: "[Intro — Call to Order]\n(Nylon guitar, brushes, one printer page feeding)\n\n[Verse 1]\nItem one: the sky is still a little open\nItem two: the bin is still the wrong blue\nItem three: the purple light on Juniper\nIs parked in a space it isn't zoned to\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nMotion to approve\nSeconded by the hum\nAll in favor of the thing we can't describe\nSay \"seasonal\" and go numb\n\n[Chorus]\nA permit for the purple light\nLaminated, hung correctly\nA permit for the purple light\nValid through the end of night\nIf it has a lanyard, it's a neighbor\nIf it glows, it glows politely\nA permit for the purple light\nPlease park it out of sight\n\n[Verse 2]\nBrad has questions, Brad is always Brad\nThe minutes note that Brad was heard\nTwo women in black lace along the back wall\nNon-voting, and they have not said a word\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nMotion to approve\nSeconded by the hum\nAll in favor of the thing we can't describe\nSay \"seasonal\" and go numb\n\n[Chorus]\nA permit for the purple light\nLaminated, hung correctly\nA permit for the purple light\nValid through the end of night\nIf it has a lanyard, it's a neighbor\nIf it glows, it glows politely\nA permit for the purple light\nPlease park it out of sight\n\n[Bridge — Roll Call]\n(Spoken, pen clicking)\nKaren. Present.\nBrad. Present, with concerns.\nThe bin. Present. Still the wrong blue.\nThe light. — The light does not respond.\nMark the light as present.\nMark the light as décor.\n\n[Final Chorus]\nA permit for the purple light\nLaminated, hung correctly\nA permit for the purple light\nRenewable, if it behaves\nIf it has a lanyard, it's a neighbor\nIf it hums, we hum along\nA permit for the purple light\nMeeting adjourned. Don't go outside.\n\n[Outro — Adjourned]\n(Vibraphone once, guitar stops mid-pattern, printer drops the last page)",
    notes: "## Production notes\n- **Episode placement:** Episode 4 bureaucracy-afterglow cutaway — plays under the HOA meeting where the portal aftermath gets reclassified as seasonal décor. Brad's bin and the purple light's parking permit are the season-arc tags, sung verbatim.\n- **Emotional arc:** Call to order → deadpan itemization → the chorus treats laminate as containment → roll call is the only place fear shows (the light does not respond) → adjourned, nobody leaves.\n- **Characters:** Karen-adjacent lead voice (the minutes, personified). Lillith and Rubella as non-voting backing unison, back wall, never featured. Brad is heard, not sung.\n- **Structural flaw:** the vibraphone hits only on \"permit\"; if Minimax spreads it, cut it entirely.\n- **Runtime target:** ~2:30. 12s chorus bumper for an EyeWash clubhouse CRT.\n- **Reference energy:** Astrud Gilberto reading a zoning variance; Stereolab office hours; the straightest possible Getz/Gilberto comp under the least possible situation.",
