    stylePrompt: "Torch-song waltz / dressing-room chamber pop in 3/4 at 72 BPM, A minor with a Picardy third that only arrives on the final word. Upright piano slightly out of tune with itself, a single cello, brushed snare with the wire loosened, a string quartet that enters only on the second chorus and is gone by the bridge. Two female voices in dialogue — Lillith dry and clipped, Rubella warm and unbothered — who never sing the downbeat together: one is always a beat early, the other a beat late, so the waltz limps like a heel that's about to give. Chorus in close harmony a third apart, mixed so you cannot tell which sister is on top. Intro is vanity-bulb hum and a lipstick cap clicking in 3. Bridge is one voice, then the other, then a mirror-image of the melody (retrograde) sung by both. Mood: elegant, bored, terrified of boredom, trading lives at a mirror while the town glows orange through the window. No drums louder than a brush. Ends on a single held note, both voices, and one bulb going out.",
